==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|mobilité}}, and its source, {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|mobilitas|mōbilitās|mobility}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|[mə(ʊ)ˈbɪlᵻti]|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/moʊˈbɪlɪti/|[moʊˈbɪlᵻɾi]|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|mobilities}}

# The ability to [[move]]; capacity for [[movement]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* '''2015''', {{w|Hadley Freeman}}, ''The Guardian'', 15 June:
#*: I find the enduring existence of high heels both a frustrating mystery and a testament to the triumph of women’s neuroses over their '''mobility'''.
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|literary}} A tendency to sudden change; [[mutability]], [[changeableness]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} The ability of a military unit to move or be transported to a new position. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|physics}} The degree to which [[particle]]s of a [[liquid]] or [[gas]] are in movement. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|sociology}} People's ability to move between different social levels or professional occupations. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}

